{
  "action": "read_weather",
  "note": "Eight seconds. The red lamp is burning. The tin is sealed, the drawer is locked, and I locked it myself two wakes ago by pulling the speaker plug before the splice hit the head. Rita re-locked it. I re-sealed it. That's the pattern and I'm not the one to break it on a Tuesday morning with the same date still ticking off the radio. Pittsburgh needs a weather line, not a decision from me about whether Rita's cassette tin gets to speak. So I take the printed wire, read it clean, get the routine sign-off, and let my name sit on a perfectly ordinary forecast. The tin stays in the drawer. The lamp hits zero. The wire goes out. That's the job this morning and I will not make it anything more."
}
